  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 233 PM EDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nCurrently-Tonight...Some drier air is nudging its way in from the \nsoutheast, with GOES-19 TPW imagery showing greater moisture (PW 1.6-\n1.7\") focused inland. This should keep a majority of isolated to \nscattered showers and storms (PoPs 20-40%) that form through late \nafternoon and toward sunset across the interior, especially along \nand west of the I-4 corridor. A prevailing southerly flow east of TS \nBertha thats centered south of the western FL panhandle, will \ntransport this activity northward through this evening, with this \nactivity diminishing by midnight. DCAPE values between 1000-1500 \nJ/kg and an the elevated southerly steering flow (15-20 kts) may \nstill allow for a few stronger storms to develop, with the main \nthreats being strong wind gusts up to 40-50 mph and frequent \nlightning. Repeated rounds of heavier showers and storms may also \nlead to localized heavy rainfall of 1-3\" in spots. \n\nMostly dry conditions then forecast overnight, with only a slight \n(20%) chance for onshore moving showers along the southern Treasure \nCoast late tonight. Remaining warm tonight, with lows for most \nlocations in the mid to upper 70s. \n\nWednesday-Thursday...TS Bertha will continue a gradual westward \nmotion through midweek, either along or just south of the northern \nGulf coast. The sfc Atlantic ridge at the same time gradually \nextends across central FL, with ridge axis lifting northward. This \nwill allow winds to weaken as they gradually become more onshore, \nbacking to the S/SE tomorrow and E/SE into Thursday. Shower and \nstorm coverage remains below normal overall through midweek, as \ndrier air east of Bertha lingers. Greatest chance for afternoon and \nevening showers and storms Wednesday (PoPs up to 30-40%) will be \nacross Okeechobee County and Treasure Coast region during the \nafternoon, and then focused west of Orlando with the sea breeze \ncollision near to after sunset. The more dominant onshore flow on \nThursday should lower rain chances even more, with only a slight \n(20%) chance of showers and storms currently forecast across the \ninterior through the afternoon/early evening. The drier air aloft \nwill still keep a potential for isolated strong storms, producing \nstrong wind gusts and frequent lightning. \n\nHighs will continue to be near to above normal, in the low 90s along \nthe coast and more widespread mid 90s across the interior. The drier \nair aloft should lower dewpoints as mixing increases into the \nafternoon. Therefore, peak heat index values are forecast to remain \nbelow Heat Advisory criteria, but should still range from 100-107F. \nThose spending extended periods of time outdoors will need to remain \nadequately hydrated and take frequent breaks in the shade or an air-\nconditioned building to avoid heat-related illness. Warm and humid \nconditions will prevail overnight, with lows continuing in the mid \nto upper 70s for much of the area. \n\nFriday-Monday...(Previous Discussion) Broad mid-level ridging will \nremain in place across the southern US late this week, with a trough \nacross the eastern US gradually digging southward towards Florida \ninto this weekend and early next week. At the surface, broad high \npressure is forecast to remain situated across the local Atlantic \nwaters on Friday, with the high gradually pushing southward this \nweekend as a low moves offshore the Carolinas and drags a frontal \nboundary southward across the southeastern US. The boundary will \nstall across north Florida on Sunday, continuing to drift southward \ntowards the peninsula into early next week as it weakens. Locally, \nlingering dry air across the area on Friday will keep rain chances a \nbit limited across the north, with increasing moisture across the \nTreasure Coast and near Lake Okeechobee causing a return of near to \nabove normal rain chances. By this weekend, a moist air mass will \noverspread the Florida peninsula and the typical diurnal summertime \npattern is forecast, with convective activity primarily being driven \nby the sea breezes and their interactions. A return of westerly flow \nwill also favor higher coverage across the eastern portions of the \npeninsula near the coast. By Monday, the moisture from the decaying \nboundary will arrive across east central Florida, with continued \nnear-normal rain chances forecast through the afternoon hours \nareawide. Overall, showers and storms are forecast to diminish each \nevening through the overnight hours, with lingering activity \npossible across the local Atlantic waters.\n\nAbove normal temperatures are forecast to continue across east \ncentral Florida through Sunday, with some indication that the \nincreasing moisture and cloud coverage will help keep temperatures \ncloser to normal on Monday afternoon. Peak heat indices will \ncontinue to exceed 100F areawide, with some locations potentially \nnearing Heat Advisory criteria. Confidence in this remains low \nthough, so will continue to monitor. Lows generally in the 70s.",
